3. Slides 2–4 — requirements and assumptions
Slide 2 — Functional requirements
          ID	  Requirement	                        Purpose
          FR-1	Request routing	                    Route requests to the correct backend service
          FR-2	Authentication	                    Verify caller identity or validate trusted credentials
          FR-3	Authorization enforcement	          Apply appropriate access policies at the API boundary
          FR-4	Rate limiting	                      Control request volume by identity, tenant, or client
          FR-5	Load balancing	                    Distribute traffic across healthy service instances
          FR-6	Request/response transformation	    Normalize headers, paths, and supported payload formats
          FR-7	Observability	                      Propagate correlation IDs and emit metrics, logs, and traces
          FR-8	API versioning	                    Route supported API versions without unnecessary client disruption

The gateway should not become the owner of order processing, payment decisions, or domain-specific business rules. 
Those remain with their respective services.

=====================================================================================================================================
Slide 3 — Non-functional requirements

    Quality	                Design objective	                                                    Engineering concern
    Availability	          Avoid one gateway instance becoming a platform-wide failure	          Redundancy and health-based routing
    Low latency	            Limit gateway overhead	                                              Efficient I/O, bounded filters, connection reuse
    Scalability	            Add capacity without redesigning the system	                          Stateless replicas and horizontal scaling
    Security	              Protect services from unauthorized access and abuse	                  TLS, identity validation, least privilege
    Resilience	            Contain slow or failed dependencies	                                  Deadlines, bulkheads, circuit breakers
    Consistency	            Enforce shared policies predictably	                                  Configuration management and policy versioning
    Operability	            Detect degradation before widespread failure	                        SLOs, telemetry, controlled rollouts

These requirements can conflict. 
For example, strict synchronous authorization can strengthen control 
but increase latency and dependency on the authorization service.


