📘 SLIDE 6 — API GATEWAY REQUEST LIFECYCLE

🔄 STEP 1: ACCEPT REQUEST
   ├── 🔐 TLS termination
   ├── 🔍 Protocol validation
   └── 📦 Request-size limits

       ↓

🛡️ STEP 2: APPLY EDGE AND IDENTITY CONTROLS
   ├── 🧱 Web Application Firewall (WAF), where deployed
   ├── 🔑 Authentication
   └── 🔒 Authorization policy enforcement

       ↓

🚦 STEP 3: APPLY TRAFFIC POLICY
   ├── 🚥 Rate limiting
   ├── 📊 Client quotas
   ├── 🔄 Concurrency controls
   └── 🚨 Abuse prevention

       ↓

🛣️ STEP 4: RESOLVE THE ROUTE
   ├── 🔀 HTTP method and request path
   ├── 🏷️ API version
   ├── 🏢 Tenant identification, where applicable
   └── 🧭 Routing metadata

       ↓

⚖️ STEP 5: SELECT A BACKEND
   ├── 🔎 Service discovery
   ├── ❤️ Backend health status
   ├── ⚖️ Load balancing
   └── 🔌 Connection pool management

       ↓

⏱️ STEP 6: EXECUTE A BOUNDED UPSTREAM CALL
   ├── ⏳ Enforce request deadlines and timeouts
   ├── 🔁 Apply carefully scoped retries
   └── 🧯 Use a circuit breaker where appropriate

       ↓

📤 STEP 7: RETURN RESPONSE AND OBSERVE
   ├── 🔄 Response mapping
   ├── 🔗 Trace context propagation
   ├── 📊 Metrics and logging
   └── 🛡️ Safe error handling

-------------------------------------------------------
🎯 END RESULT
The client receives a controlled response, while the
gateway enforces security, traffic policies, routing,
resilience and observability across the request lifecycle.
-------------------------------------------------------

This is a logical sequence, not a requirement that all processing be implemented
as seven separate network hops. Many stages are local filters 
inside the same gateway process.



